@@ -205,6 +205,7 @@ Legend: `[x]` done in code · `[~]` partly done / needs your device to confirm 
 | 0243 | 2026-11-15 | Companion mode, patch 8 (your request: "go for Companion 0182"). **Stock actions from Scan.** On a Stock check opened from the companion's Scan tab, a **Receive, adjust or count** button under the result opens a panel with the same three modes, reasons and keypad as Inventory. It asks for the PIN (`requireStepUp('shopify')`), writes through `adjustStock`/`setStock`, and updates the local count. Tapping one of the other variations switches to it. A retry after a dropped connection reuses the same idempotency key, so Shopify applies it once. Training changes this device only. The checkout grid's Stock check is unchanged (the panel is off there). New `src/lib/stockAction.ts` (pure plan, summary and retry key), `src/screens/StockActionPanel.tsx`, `tests/stockaction.test.ts` (7 tests), a line in the Device mode Help page, `COMPANION_PLAN.md` C22, `QUEUE.md`, `TO_DO.md`, `DEVICE_TESTS.md` T115. Not compiled here (no `tsc`); needs a device test. |
 | 0244 | 2026-11-15 | Stock stays in step (your request: share one Shopify stock count between the devices, and refresh the catalogue and stock half-hourly). First your question about whether each iPad pushed an absolute stock number after a sale: **it does not**. A sale creates a Shopify order with `DECREMENT_IGNORING_POLICY` at the register's location, so Shopify subtracts and two iPads selling together both count; the poll only reads stock. Only Count and starting stock send an absolute number. **Shared location (iCloud only):** `locationId` and `locationName` join the CloudKit shared settings (`sharedSettings.ts`), so changing the location on one iPad moves them all; the Shopify-metaobject path still keeps it per device. **Half-hourly refresh:** `refreshCatalogue` in `sync.ts` re-reads products, stock, collections and discounts (not customers) when the last read is 30 minutes old, the location differs from the one last read, and no sales are queued (`src/lib/catalogueRefresh.ts`). New `tests/cataloguerefresh.test.ts`, a shared-location test, `T116`, Help edits. Also type-checked the whole project for the first time here (the missing packages installed): no errors in `src/`, and all 1985 tests pass, including 0243's. |
 | 0245 | 2026-11-15 | Stock running out warning (your request: a feature like the cart warnings, for an item the current order would leave at 0 or below). **A built-in extra page on the Charge warnings.** `src/lib/stockWarning.ts`: `stockShortfalls` adds up the lines of each tracked variation and keeps those whose stock minus the quantity is 0 or lower (untracked, custom, gift card and unknown items never count); `stockWarningPage` shapes it as a normal cart warning page, so `CartWarningsSheet` and both checkouts show it with the same Next/Back, tick-to-continue and "not asked again unless it changes" behaviour (the page text carries the numbers, so a changed cart brings it back). It is appended after the shop's own warnings in `useCartWarningGate`. Setting `stockWarning { enabled, requireAcknowledgement }`, **off by default**, in Settings ▸ Cart warnings, shared on iCloud (a new CloudKit shared key; the Shopify-metaobject set is unchanged). Not a queue-mode warning: it only appears at Charge. `tests/stockwarning.test.ts` (7), shared-settings tests, Help page `sales/cart-warnings`, `T117`. `tsc` clean in `src/`. |
+| 0246 | 2026-11-15 | Set up a new device by QR, no iCloud (your request: QR-transferable settings and data for setting up new terminals and companions; you chose the one-QR setup, offered on first run). **The set up device** (Settings ▸ Backup & transfer ▸ **Show setup QR**, admin PIN first) seals its ordinary backup (`collectBackup`, the same settings, staff, grid and logins as a backup file) with `encryptBackup` using a fresh 16-symbol one-time code (80 bits), serves it on the shop Wi-Fi from the same small server as the Square history share (`answerRequest`, now with `lanIp` and `askForIp` exported), and stops after the first successful transfer, 5 minutes, leaving the page or the app going to the background. The QR is `POSSETUP1|ip|port|key|code`: the address, a random path key and the code, so Wi-Fi only ever carries ciphertext. **The new device** (a row on Settings ▸ Backup & transfer and at the top of the first-run Set up this device page) scans it, confirms (naming the address), asks the admin PIN if one exists, fetches (`assistantFetch`, falling back to a raw socket), decrypts with the code, `validateBackup`, `applyBackup`, then loads the catalogue with `importFromShopify`. It only follows private Wi-Fi addresses (10., 172.16 to 31., 192.168.), and device-only settings (name, mode, features, register id) are never overwritten (`DEVICE_KEYS`). New `src/lib/setupTransfer.ts`, `setupTransferIO.ts`, `src/screens/SetupTransfer.tsx`, `tests/setuptransfer.test.ts` (7), Help pages `settings/backup` and `start/setup`, `T118`. Not included: Square history (still its own QR, 0150) and the Zeller pairing. `tsc` clean in `src/`. |
 | 0241 | 2026-11-14 | **B7e, Help pictures for three more pages (your request).** The picture engine (`src/lib/helpFigures.ts`) already drew 12 pages; this adds seven figures drawn from the real screens' labels: Cloud sync (set up, invite link, join, Back up everything and Export all events, which the page did not describe at all), Offline and sync errors (the Support & diagnostics Sync rows, with the new **Last full sync** line explained) and Split and part payments (the Split amount sheet and the Payment screen after a split). 15 of 68 pages now have a figure. No code; `help.json` rebuilt, the 63 help tests pass. Not looked at on a device: the pictures follow the screens' labels but I cannot see the iPad. The other 53 pages are not drawn; the Daily float report is the hardest (it draws notes and coins) and was left. | B7e |
 | 0240 | 2026-11-14 | **B8, review of the gift-card calls before the live test (your request).** Read `giftcards.ts` against Shopify's `giftCardCredit` and `giftCardDebit` pages: the mutations, fields and the `write_gift_card_transactions` scope are as documented. Shopify gives neither call an idempotency key, and the cancel side already looked in the card's history before crediting again, but the **charge did not**: if Shopify took the charge and the reply was lost, the app said "could not be charged", the cashier tapped again and the card was charged twice. New `debitGiftCardOnce` and `src/lib/shopify/giftCardOnce.ts`: the Nth payment of a sale has the same note on every retry (`POS sale <8 chars> tender N`), the card's history is read for it first, and a debit already there is not sent again (Shopify returns a debit as a negative amount, so amounts are compared by size). The credit side now uses the same helper. Pay.tsx charges through it. `tests/giftcardonce.test.ts` (6 tests) pass. DEVICE_TESTS T10 gains the Wi-Fi-off charge test and a check of the notes. **The live run is still yours:** it spends real gift-card value on your store. | B8 |
 | 0239 | 2026-11-11 | **iCloud was slow, and polls could pile up.** Found by reading the code, not measured on a device: the 25 second tick called `pollShared()` straight away with no check for one already running, and the change pushes and the Sync now button call it too. One poll is about twenty iCloud round trips in a row, so on a slow connection it takes longer than 25 seconds and every tick started another, all competing for iCloud and rewriting the same local state; a cash drawer closed on one iPad then took minutes to show on the other. `pollShared` is now single-flight (`src/lib/singleFlight.ts`): a call made while a poll runs joins it. Diagnostics ▸ Sync gains a **Last full sync** line with the total time and the slowest parts (`src/lib/pollTiming.ts`), so the next step is based on real numbers. Not changed, and likely still slow: the poll's steps run one after another, Back up everything and the events export read 100 records per round trip, and the iCloud tab asks iCloud four things in a row to connect. Parallelising the steps is the next lever but needs a look at what each writes first. Not run on a device. `tests/companionjobs`, `logosync` and `noticesync` cannot run in the sandbox (missing modules) and fail the same without this change; their source checks were run by hand and hold. | Sync |
